Q: Bind an entire control instead of individual properties in C# WPF .NET 8 A: <p>I was able to finally get it to work.</p>
<p>Here's the code that made it work.</p>
<p>MainWindow.xaml:</p>
<pre><code> &lt;TextBlock x:Name=&quot;TextBlock_DBG&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>MainWindow.xaml.cs</p>
<pre><code>    public partial class MainWindow : Window
    {
        public TestClass TestClass { get; set; }
        public MainWindow()
        {
            InitializeComponent();
            TestClass = new TestClass();
            TestClass.TextBlock_DBG = TextBlock_DBG;
            TestClass.TextBlock_DBG.Text = &quot;Debug Message TC&quot;;
        }

        private void Button_Click(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
        {
            TestClass.DebugMsgButton();
        }

        private void Button_Click_1(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
        {
            TestClass.ChangeBackgroundColor();

        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>TestClass.cs</p>
<pre><code>    public class TestClass  //: INotifyPropertyChanged
    {

        public TextBlock TextBlock_DBG { get; set; }

        public void DebugMsgButton()
        {
            this.TextBlock_DBG.Text += &quot;\ntest&quot;;
        }
        public void ChangeBackgroundColor()
        {
            Debug.WriteLine(&quot;[TestClass.cs] FUNCTION CALL: ChangeBackgroundColor&quot;);
            this.TextBlock_DBG.Background = System.Windows.Media.Brushes.Red;
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>This is all I need. I give the control a name, and in the testclass, I create an internal control with the same type as the object and then from the MainWindow.xaml.cs I create a reference between the object and the class's internal object.</p>
<p>This is exactly the way I expected it to work, but didn't get it to work, then research lead me into that I had to bind properties, but luckily, that's not the case.</p>
